args.content:
# Spec: `atlas schema inspect` (community/dev build at `/tmp/atlas_orig`)

All observations were made by running the binary through its normal CLI only.
Unless stated otherwise: output was captured piped (non-TTY), exit code shown as `exit=N`.
Conventions below: stdout/stderr are shown separately; every fenced block is byte-verbatim.

## Global facts

- Version identity (`atlas version`, all on **stdout**, exit 0, stderr empty, trailing newline after last line):

```text
atlas unofficial version - development
https://github.com/ariga/atlas/releases/latest
To download an official version, visit: https://atlasgo.io/getting-started#installation
```

- **Community-edition / unofficial-build notice**: there is **no** separate notice printed by
  `schema inspect` (or by `--help` of any probed command) — not on stdout, not on stderr,
  not under a TTY, not on errors. The only notice-like text in the whole CLI surface observed
  is the `atlas version` output above ("unofficial version - development" + "To download an
  official version..."), which goes to **stdout** only, and only for the `version` command.
  `atlas license` prints a short Apache-2.0 note on stdout, exit 0, nothing on stderr.
- **Errors**: always printed to **stderr** as a single line `Error: <message>\n`, exit code **1**,
  stdout empty. (Cobra-style usage text is NOT printed on errors — only the `Error:` line.)
- **TTY vs piped**: no difference for `schema inspect`. Under `script -qec ... /dev/null` the
  bytes are identical (modulo the terminal's `\n`→`\r\n` translation). No colors, no spinner,
  no prompts — inspect never prompts.
- No trailing whitespace on any output line; HCL output contains no blank lines.
- `atlas help schema inspect`, `atlas schema inspect -h`, and `atlas schema inspect --help`
  produce byte-identical output.

## `atlas schema inspect --help` (verbatim)

stdout, exit 0, stderr empty:

```text
'atlas schema inspect' connects to the given database and inspects its schema.
It then prints to the screen the schema of that database in Atlas DDL syntax. This output can be
saved to a file, commonly by redirecting the output to a file named with a ".hcl" suffix:

  atlas schema inspect -u "mysql://user:pass@localhost:3306/dbname" > schema.hcl

This file can then be edited and used with the `atlas schema apply` command to plan
and execute schema migrations against the given database. In cases where users wish to inspect
all multiple schemas in a given database (for instance a MySQL server may contain multiple named
databases), omit the relevant part from the url, e.g. "mysql://user:pass@localhost:3306/".
To select specific schemas from the databases, users may use the "--schema" (or "-s" shorthand)
flag.

Usage:
  atlas schema inspect [flags]

Examples:
  atlas schema inspect -u "mysql://user:pass@localhost:3306/dbname"
  atlas schema inspect -u "mariadb://user:pass@localhost:3306/" --schema=schemaA,schemaB -s schemaC
  atlas schema inspect --url "postgres://user:pass@host:port/dbname?sslmode=disable"
  atlas schema inspect -u "sqlite://file:ex1.db?_fk=1"

Flags:
  -u, --url string        [driver://username:password@address/dbname?param=value] select a resource using the URL format
      --dev-url string    [driver://username:password@address/dbname?param=value] select a dev database using the URL format
  -s, --schema strings    set schema names
      --exclude strings   list of glob patterns used to filter resources from applying
      --format string     Go template to use to format the output
  -h, --help              help for inspect

Global Flags:
  -c, --config string        select config (project) file using URL format (default "file://atlas.hcl")
      --env string           set which env from the config file to use
      --var <name>=<value>   input variables (default [])
```

Parent `atlas schema --help` (stdout, exit 0):

```text
The `atlas schema` command groups subcommands working with declarative Atlas schemas.

Usage:
  atlas schema [command]

Available Commands:
  apply       Apply an atlas schema to a target database.
  clean       Removes all objects from the connected database.
  diff        Calculate and print the diff between two schemas.
  fmt         Formats Atlas HCL files
  inspect     Inspect a database and print its schema in Atlas DDL syntax.

Flags:
  -c, --config string        select config (project) file using URL format (default "file://atlas.hcl")
      --env string           set which env from the config file to use
  -h, --help                 help for schema
      --var <name>=<value>   input variables (default [])

Use "atlas schema [command] --help" for more information about a command.
```

## Empty sqlite database

Command: `atlas schema inspect -u "sqlite://t.db"` where `t.db` does not exist beforehand.

- Side effect: the file `t.db` **is created** as a **0-byte** file.
- stdout (exit 0, stderr empty), ends with a single trailing newline:

```hcl
schema "main" {
}
```

- `sqlite://:memory:`, `sqlite://file?mode=memory`, and even bare `sqlite://` all produce the
  identical output (`schema "main" {\n}\n`), exit 0.

## Rich schema #1: PKs, AUTOINCREMENT, composite PK, NOT NULL, UNIQUE, DEFAULTs, CHECK

Database created with python3 sqlite3:

```sql
CREATE TABLE users (
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
  name TEXT NOT NULL,
  email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
  age INTEGER DEFAULT 18,
  bio TEXT DEFAULT 'hello ''world''',
  created_at TEXT DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
  score REAL DEFAULT 1.5,
  active INTEGER DEFAULT 1,
  CONSTRAINT age_check CHECK (age >= 0)
);
CREATE TABLE posts (
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
  user_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
  title TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'untitled',
  body TEXT,
  UNIQUE (user_id, title)
);
CREATE TABLE composite (
  a INTEGER NOT NULL,
  b TEXT NOT NULL,
  c BLOB,
  PRIMARY KEY (a, b)
);
```

Command: `atlas schema inspect -u "sqlite://rich1.db"` — exit 0, stderr empty, stdout verbatim:

```hcl
table "users" {
  schema = schema.main
  column "id" {
    null           = true
    type           = integer
    auto_increment = true
  }
  column "name" {
    null = false
    type = text
  }
  column "email" {
    null = false
    type = text
  }
  column "age" {
    null    = true
    type    = integer
    default = 18
  }
  column "bio" {
    null    = true
    type    = text
    default = "hello 'world'"
  }
  column "created_at" {
    null    = true
    type    = text
    default = sql("CURRENT_TIMESTAMP")
  }
  column "score" {
    null    = true
    type    = real
    default = 1.5
  }
  column "active" {
    null    = true
    type    = integer
    default = 1
  }
  primary_key {
    columns = [column.id]
  }
  index "sqlite_autoindex_users_1" {
    unique  = true
    columns = [column.email]
  }
  check "age_check" {
    expr = "(age >= 0)"
  }
}
table "posts" {
  schema = schema.main
  column "id" {
    null = true
    type = integer
  }
  column "user_id" {
    null = false
    type = integer
  }
  column "title" {
    null    = false
    type    = text
    default = "untitled"
  }
  column "body" {
    null = true
    type = text
  }
  primary_key {
    columns = [column.id]
  }
  index "sqlite_autoindex_posts_1" {
    unique  = true
    columns = [column.user_id, column.title]
  }
}
table "composite" {
  schema = schema.main
  column "a" {
    null = false
    type = integer
  }
  column "b" {
    null = false
    type = text
  }
  column "c" {
    null = true
    type = blob
  }
  primary_key {
    columns = [column.a, column.b]
  }
}
schema "main" {
}
```

HCL formatting rules observed:

- Tables are emitted in creation order, then `schema "main" {\n}` last. No blank lines anywhere.
- Every column block always has `null` (explicit `true`/`false`) and `type`.
- `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY` (without AUTOINCREMENT) → `null = true`, plain `primary_key` block
  (see `posts.id`); `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT` → `null = true` plus
  `auto_increment = true` (see `users.id`).
- SQLite UNIQUE constraints surface as indexes named after sqlite's autoindex
  (`sqlite_autoindex_<table>_<n>`), `unique = true`, `columns = [...]`.
- DEFAULTs: numeric literals unquoted (`default = 18`, `default = 1.5`); string literals quoted
  with embedded quotes unescaped (`default = "hello 'world'"`); `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` →
  `default = sql("CURRENT_TIMESTAMP")`.
- CHECK constraint: `check "age_check" { expr = "(age >= 0)" }` — expression includes the
  original parentheses.
- Attribute alignment: `=` signs are aligned by padding within each block (gofmt-style, e.g.
  `null           = true` when the block also has `auto_increment`).
- Blocks inside a table appear in order: columns (definition order), `primary_key`,
  `foreign_key`s, `index`es, `check`s (see rich2 for FKs).

## Rich schema #2: FKs with actions, indexes (unique/partial/expression/desc)

Database DDL:

```sql
PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;
CREATE TABLE parent (
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
  code TEXT UNIQUE
);
CREATE TABLE child (
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
  parent_id INTEGER REFERENCES parent(id) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE SET NULL,
  parent_code TEXT,
  FOREIGN KEY (parent_code) REFERENCES parent(code) ON DELETE SET DEFAULT
);
CREATE INDEX idx_child_pid ON child(parent_id);
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_child_code ON child(parent_code);
CREATE INDEX idx_partial ON child(parent_id) WHERE parent_id > 10;
CREATE INDEX idx_expr ON child(parent_id + 1);
CREATE INDEX idx_desc ON child(id DESC);
```

Command: `atlas schema inspect -u "sqlite://rich2.db"` — exit 0, stdout verbatim:

```hcl
table "parent" {
  schema = schema.main
  column "id" {
    null = true
    type = integer
  }
  column "code" {
    null = true
    type = text
  }
  primary_key {
    columns = [column.id]
  }
  index "sqlite_autoindex_parent_1" {
    unique  = true
    columns = [column.code]
  }
}
table "child" {
  schema = schema.main
  column "id" {
    null = true
    type = integer
  }
  column "parent_id" {
    null = true
    type = integer
  }
  column "parent_code" {
    null = true
    type = text
  }
  primary_key {
    columns = [column.id]
  }
  foreign_key "0" {
    columns     = [column.parent_code]
    ref_columns = [table.parent.column.code]
    on_update   = NO_ACTION
    on_delete   = SET_DEFAULT
  }
  foreign_key "1" {
    columns     = [column.parent_id]
    ref_columns = [table.parent.column.id]
    on_update   = SET_NULL
    on_delete   = CASCADE
  }
  index "idx_child_pid" {
    columns = [column.parent_id]
  }
  index "idx_child_code" {
    unique  = true
    columns = [column.parent_code]
  }
  index "idx_partial" {
    columns = [column.parent_id]
    where   = "parent_id > 10"
  }
  index "idx_expr" {
    on {
      expr = "parent_id + 1"
    }
  }
  index "idx_desc" {
    on {
      desc   = true
      column = column.id
    }
  }
}
schema "main" {
}
```

Notes:

- FKs are **unnamed in sqlite**, so atlas names them `"0"`, `"1"` following sqlite's
  `PRAGMA foreign_key_list` ids (FK id 0 = the table-level `FOREIGN KEY` clause on
  `parent_code`, id 1 = the inline `REFERENCES` on `parent_id` — i.e. reverse of DDL order).
- `on_update`/`on_delete` are always emitted, unquoted enum-like values:
  `NO_ACTION`, `SET_DEFAULT`, `SET_NULL`, `CASCADE`.
- Partial index → `where = "parent_id > 10"`.
- Expression index → `on { expr = "parent_id + 1" }` (no `columns` attr).
- DESC column index → `on { desc = true\n column = column.id }` (attribute order: `desc`, then `column`).

## Views and triggers — NOT emitted

Database DDL:

```sql
CREATE TABLE t1 (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, val TEXT);
CREATE VIEW v1 AS SELECT id, val FROM t1 WHERE id > 0;
CREATE TRIGGER trg1 AFTER INSERT ON t1 BEGIN UPDATE t1 SET val = NEW.val WHERE id = NEW.id; END;
CREATE VIEW v2(x, y) AS SELECT id, val FROM t1;
```

`atlas schema inspect -u "sqlite://rich3.db"` (exit 0) prints **only the table** —
views and triggers are completely absent from the default HCL output, and also absent from
`{{ sql . }}`, `{{ json . }}`, and `{{ mermaid . }}` output:

```hcl
table "t1" {
  schema = schema.main
  column "id" {
    null = true
    type = integer
  }
  column "val" {
    null = true
    type = text
  }
  primary_key {
    columns = [column.id]
  }
}
schema "main" {
}
```

## Type mapping, generated columns, WITHOUT ROWID

DDL: `CREATE TABLE types (a VARCHAR(255), b DECIMAL(10,2), c NUMERIC, d BOOLEAN, e DATETIME,
f DOUBLE PRECISION, g CHAR(10), h CLOB, i INT2, j BIGINT, k UNSIGNED BIG INT, l VARCHAR, m,
n INTEGER GENERATED ALWAYS AS (a) VIRTUAL, o TEXT GENERATED ALWAYS AS ('x') STORED);`
plus `CREATE TABLE norowid (x INT, y TEXT, PRIMARY KEY(x, y)) WITHOUT ROWID;`

Output (exit 0):

```hcl
table "types" {
  schema = schema.main
  column "a" {
    null = true
    type = varchar(255)
  }
  column "b" {
    null = true
    type = decimal(10,2)
  }
  column "c" {
    null = true
    type = numeric
  }
  column "d" {
    null = true
    type = boolean
  }
  column "e" {
    null = true
    type = datetime
  }
  column "f" {
    null = true
    type = double_precision
  }
  column "g" {
    null = true
    type = sql("char")
  }
  column "h" {
    null = true
    type = clob
  }
  column "i" {
    null = true
    type = int2
  }
  column "j" {
    null = true
    type = bigint
  }
  column "k" {
    null = true
    type = unsigned_big_int
  }
  column "l" {
    null = true
    type = varchar
  }
  column "m" {
    null = true
    type = blob
  }
  column "n" {
    null = true
    type = integer
    as {
      expr = "(a)"
      type = VIRTUAL
    }
  }
  column "o" {
    null = true
    type = text
    as {
      expr = "('x')"
      type = STORED
    }
  }
}
table "norowid" {
  schema = schema.main
  column "x" {
    null = false
    type = int
  }
  column "y" {
    null = false
    type = text
  }
  primary_key {
    columns = [column.x, column.y]
  }
  without_rowid = true
}
schema "main" {
}
```

Notes: type names are lowercased, spaces become underscores (`double_precision`,
`unsigned_big_int`); `CHAR(10)` loses its size and falls back to `type = sql("char")`;
a typeless column (`m`) becomes `blob`; generated columns get an `as { expr/type }` block
(`expr` includes original parentheses, `type = VIRTUAL|STORED` unquoted); `WITHOUT ROWID`
→ table-level `without_rowid = true` after the primary_key block.

## `--format` templates

### `{{ sql . }}`

Command: `atlas schema inspect -u "sqlite://rich1.db" --format '{{ sql . }}'` — exit 0,
trailing newline present. Note: sqlite autoindex names are **regenerated** as
`<table>_<col>[_<col>...]` in this output (unlike HCL, which keeps `sqlite_autoindex_*`):

```sql
-- Create "users" table
CREATE TABLE `users` (`id` integer NULL PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, `name` text NOT NULL, `email` text NOT NULL, `age` integer NULL DEFAULT 18, `bio` text NULL DEFAULT 'hello ''world''', `created_at` text NULL DEFAULT (CURRENT_TIMESTAMP), `score` real NULL DEFAULT 1.5, `active` integer NULL DEFAULT 1, CONSTRAINT `age_check` CHECK (age >= 0));
-- Create index "users_email" to table: "users"
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX `users_email` ON `users` (`email`);
-- Create "posts" table
CREATE TABLE `posts` (`id` integer NULL, `user_id` integer NOT NULL, `title` text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'untitled', `body` text NULL, PRIMARY KEY (`id`));
-- Create index "posts_user_id_title" to table: "posts"
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX `posts_user_id_title` ON `posts` (`user_id`, `title`);
-- Create "composite" table
CREATE TABLE `composite` (`a` integer NOT NULL, `b` text NOT NULL, `c` blob NULL, PRIMARY KEY (`a`, `b`));
```

For rich2 (FKs/indexes):

```sql
-- Create "parent" table
CREATE TABLE `parent` (`id` integer NULL, `code` text NULL, PRIMARY KEY (`id`));
-- Create index "parent_code" to table: "parent"
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX `parent_code` ON `parent` (`code`);
-- Create "child" table
CREATE TABLE `child` (`id` integer NULL, `parent_id` integer NULL, `parent_code` text NULL, PRIMARY KEY (`id`), CONSTRAINT `0` FOREIGN KEY (`parent_code`) REFERENCES `parent` (`code`) ON UPDATE NO ACTION ON DELETE SET DEFAULT, CONSTRAINT `1` FOREIGN KEY (`parent_id`) REFERENCES `parent` (`id`) ON UPDATE SET NULL ON DELETE CASCADE);
-- Create index "idx_child_pid" to table: "child"
CREATE INDEX `idx_child_pid` ON `child` (`parent_id`);
-- Create index "idx_child_code" to table: "child"
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX `idx_child_code` ON `child` (`parent_code`);
-- Create index "idx_partial" to table: "child"
CREATE INDEX `idx_partial` ON `child` (`parent_id`) WHERE parent_id > 10;
-- Create index "idx_expr" to table: "child"
CREATE INDEX `idx_expr` ON `child` ((parent_id + 1));
-- Create index "idx_desc" to table: "child"
CREATE INDEX `idx_desc` ON `child` (`id` DESC);
```

Formatting rules: backtick-quoted identifiers; each statement preceded by a `-- Create ...`
comment line; every column rendered with explicit `NULL`/`NOT NULL`; FK constraints inlined in
CREATE TABLE as `CONSTRAINT \`<n>\` FOREIGN KEY ... ON UPDATE <A> ON DELETE <A>`; expression
index wraps the expression in an extra pair of parens (`((parent_id + 1))`); empty database →
**empty stdout** (0 bytes), exit 0.

### `{{ json . }}`

Single-line JSON, **no trailing newline**. For rich1:

```json
{"schemas":[{"name":"main","tables":[{"name":"users","columns":[{"name":"id","type":"INTEGER","null":true},{"name":"name","type":"TEXT"},{"name":"email","type":"TEXT"},{"name":"age","type":"INTEGER","null":true},{"name":"bio","type":"TEXT","null":true},{"name":"created_at","type":"TEXT","null":true},{"name":"score","type":"REAL","null":true},{"name":"active","type":"INTEGER","null":true}],"indexes":[{"name":"sqlite_autoindex_users_1","unique":true,"parts":[{"column":"email"}]}],"primary_key":{"parts":[{"column":"id"}]}},{"name":"posts","columns":[{"name":"id","type":"INTEGER","null":true},{"name":"user_id","type":"INTEGER"},{"name":"title","type":"TEXT"},{"name":"body","type":"TEXT","null":true}],"indexes":[{"name":"sqlite_autoindex_posts_1","unique":true,"parts":[{"column":"user_id"},{"column":"title"}]}],"primary_key":{"parts":[{"column":"id"}]}},{"name":"composite","columns":[{"name":"a","type":"INTEGER"},{"name":"b","type":"TEXT"},{"name":"c","type":"BLOB","null":true}],"primary_key":{"parts":[{"column":"a"},{"column":"b"}]}}]}]}
```

For rich2 (shows FK/index-part encoding):

```json
{"schemas":[{"name":"main","tables":[{"name":"parent","columns":[{"name":"id","type":"INTEGER","null":true},{"name":"code","type":"TEXT","null":true}],"indexes":[{"name":"sqlite_autoindex_parent_1","unique":true,"parts":[{"column":"code"}]}],"primary_key":{"parts":[{"column":"id"}]}},{"name":"child","columns":[{"name":"id","type":"INTEGER","null":true},{"name":"parent_id","type":"INTEGER","null":true},{"name":"parent_code","type":"TEXT","null":true}],"indexes":[{"name":"idx_child_pid","parts":[{"column":"parent_id"}]},{"name":"idx_child_code","unique":true,"parts":[{"column":"parent_code"}]},{"name":"idx_partial","parts":[{"column":"parent_id"}]},{"name":"idx_expr","parts":[{"expr":"parent_id + 1"}]},{"name":"idx_desc","parts":[{"desc":true,"column":"id"}]}],"primary_key":{"parts":[{"column":"id"}]},"foreign_keys":[{"name":"0","columns":["parent_code"],"references":{"table":"parent","columns":["code"]}},{"name":"1","columns":["parent_id"],"references":{"table":"parent","columns":["id"]}}]}]}]}
```

JSON rules observed: types are UPPERCASE raw sqlite types; `"null":true` only present when
nullable; **no** defaults, **no** checks, **no** FK on_update/on_delete actions, **no** partial
`where`, no auto_increment flag; key order per table: `name`, `columns`, `indexes`,
`primary_key`, `foreign_keys` (absent keys omitted); index parts: `{"column":"x"}`,
`{"expr":"..."}`, or `{"desc":true,"column":"x"}`; empty database →
`{"schemas":[{"name":"main"}]}` (no trailing newline).

### `{{ mermaid . }}`

For rich2 (exit 0, trailing newline present):

```text
erDiagram
    parent {
      integer id PK
      text code
    }
    child {
      integer id PK
      integer parent_id FK
      text parent_code FK
    }
    child |o--o| parent : 0
    child }o--o| parent : 1
```

Rules: 4-space indent for table blocks, 6-space for attributes; type lowercased, then column
name, then ` PK` / ` FK` markers; one relation line per FK labeled with the FK name (`0`, `1`);
relation cardinality `|o--o|` when the FK column is nullable and `}o--o|`... observed: FK `0`
(nullable `parent_code`) → `|o--o|`, FK `1` (nullable `parent_id`) → `}o--o|` — the difference
tracked the second FK here; reimplementation should treat the exact cardinality rule as
emitted-per-FK from the observed sample. Single-table db (rich3):

```text
erDiagram
    t1 {
      integer id PK
      text val
    }
```

Empty database → literally `erDiagram\n`.

### Template errors

- Unknown function: `--format '{{ bad . }}'` → exit 1, stdout empty, stderr:

```text
Error: parse log format: template: format:1: function "bad" not defined
```

- Unclosed action: `--format '{{ sql '` → exit 1, stderr:

```text
Error: parse log format: template: format:1: unclosed action
```

- A plain literal template (`--format 'hello'`) prints `hello` with **no trailing newline**,
  exit 0. (The `sql`/`json`/`mermaid` functions add their own trailing newline except `json`.)

## `--schema` / `-s` and `--exclude` flags (sqlite behavior)

- `--schema main`, `-s nosuch`, `-s main -s other`: all exit 0 and produce **output identical
  to no filter** — for sqlite the schema filter is effectively a no-op (nonexistent schema names
  are silently ignored; no error).
- `--exclude <glob>` matches against **unqualified table names**:
  - `--exclude 'child'` → only `table "parent"` + `schema "main"` remain.
  - `--exclude 'ch*'` → same (glob works).
  - `--exclude 'child' --exclude 'parent'` (repeatable flag) → only `schema "main" {\n}` remains.
  - `--exclude '*'` → only `schema "main" {\n}` remains (the schema block itself is never removed).
  - `--exclude 'main.*'`, `--exclude '*.child'`, `--exclude 'child.*'`, `--exclude 'main'` →
    **no effect**, full output (patterns are not matched schema-qualified, and the schema
    itself is not excludable).

## URL variants and connection errors

All errors: exit 1, stdout empty, message on stderr as `Error: ...\n`.

| Command | Result |
|---|---|
| `-u sqlite://t.db` (missing file) | creates 0-byte `t.db`, prints `schema "main" {\n}` |
| `-u sqlite://:memory:` | `schema "main" {\n}`, exit 0 |
| `-u sqlite://file?mode=memory` | `schema "main" {\n}`, exit 0 |
| `-u sqlite://` (bare) | `schema "main" {\n}`, exit 0 |
| `-u sqlite:///tmp/probe/rich2.db` (absolute path, 3 slashes) | works, full output |
| `-u "sqlite://file:memdb1?mode=memory&cache=shared"` | `Error: invalid port ":memdb1" after host` (the `file:` DSN form does not survive URL parsing) |
| `-u sqlite://rich2.db?_fk=1` | output identical to without `?_fk=1` |
| `-u sqlite://nodir/x.db` (missing dir) | `Error: sqlite: querying schemas: unable to open database file: no such file or directory` (file NOT created) |
| `-u sqlite://corrupt.db` (junk bytes) | `Error: sqlite: querying schemas: file is not a database` |
| `-u mysql://localhost:3306/db` | `Error: mysql: query system variables: dial tcp [::1]:3306: connect: connection refused` |
| `-u mysql://root:pass@localhost:3306/db` | same `[::1]` message |
| `-u mysql://` | `Error: mysql: query system variables: dial tcp 127.0.0.1:3306: connect: connection refused` |
| `-u postgres://localhost:5432/db` | `Error: postgres: scanning system variables: dial tcp [::1]:5432: connect: connection refused` |
| `-u postgres://` | `Error: postgres: scanning system variables: missing "=" after "postgres:" in connection info string"` (note the stray trailing `"` in the message) |
| `-u foo://bar` | `Error: sql/sqlclient: unknown driver "foo". See: https://atlasgo.io/url` |

(The mysql/postgres dial addresses depend on how `localhost` resolves; on this host it tried
`[::1]` first for host-form URLs and `127.0.0.1` for the empty-host form.)

## CLI argument errors

| Command | exit | stderr |
|---|---|---|
| `atlas schema inspect` (no `-u`) | 1 | `Error: required flag(s) "url" not set` |
| `atlas schema inspect -u sqlite://t.db --badflag` | 1 | `Error: unknown flag: --badflag` |
| `atlas schema inspect -u sqlite://t.db extrapositional` | 0 | **positional args silently ignored**, normal output |

In all error cases stdout is empty and no usage text is printed.

